import Numlib.Analysis.Convex.RelativeInterior
import NumlibSurface.Rockafellar.Chapter01.Section02

/-!
# Rockafellar, Section 6: Relative interiors of convex sets

This module formalizes Section 6 of [rockafellar1970convex]: the relative interior
$\operatorname{ri} C$ and the closure $\operatorname{cl} C$ of a convex set
$C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$. The relative interior is the interior of $C$ relative to its affine hull
$\operatorname{aff} C$. The section proves the line segment principle, shows that
$\operatorname{cl} C$ and $\operatorname{ri} C$ are convex with the same affine hull as $C$ and that
a non-empty convex set has a non-empty relative interior, gives the prolongation criterion for
$\operatorname{ri} C$, and computes $\operatorname{ri}$ and $\operatorname{cl}$ of intersections,
linear images and inverse images, products, the cone over a convex set, and convex hulls of unions.
The proofs specialize the backbone module `Numlib.Analysis.Convex.RelativeInterior`.

All 18 numbered results of Section 6 are formalized; Theorem 6.9 only in the case of two sets.

## Main definitions

* Definition of relative boundary, `Rockafellar.relbd`: the set $\operatorname{cl} C \setminus
  \operatorname{ri} C$.
* Definition of relatively open set, `Rockafellar.IsRelativelyOpen`: $\operatorname{ri} C = C$.
* Corollary 6.8.1, `Rockafellar.coneOver`: the convex cone in $\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$ generated by
  $\{(1, x) \mid x \in C\}$.

## Main results

* Theorem 6.1, `Rockafellar.theorem_6_1`: the line segment principle, $(1 - \lambda) x + \lambda y
  \in \operatorname{ri} C$ for $x \in \operatorname{ri} C$, $y \in \operatorname{cl} C$ and
  $0 \le \lambda < 1$.
* Theorem 6.2, `Rockafellar.theorem_6_2_convex_cl`, `Rockafellar.theorem_6_2_convex_ri`,
  `Rockafellar.theorem_6_2_aff_cl`, `Rockafellar.theorem_6_2_aff_ri`,
  `Rockafellar.theorem_6_2_dim_cl`, `Rockafellar.theorem_6_2_dim_ri`,
  `Rockafellar.theorem_6_2_nonempty`: $\operatorname{cl} C$ and $\operatorname{ri} C$ are convex and
  have the same affine hull and dimension as $C$, and $\operatorname{ri} C \neq \emptyset$ if
  $C \neq \emptyset$.
* Theorem 6.3, `Rockafellar.theorem_6_3_cl_ri`, `Rockafellar.theorem_6_3_ri_cl`:
  $\operatorname{cl}(\operatorname{ri} C) = \operatorname{cl} C$ and
  $\operatorname{ri}(\operatorname{cl} C) = \operatorname{ri} C$.
* Corollary 6.3.1, `Rockafellar.corollary_6_3_1`, `Rockafellar.corollary_6_3_1_sandwich`: two convex
  sets have the same closure if and only if they have the same relative interior, if and only if
  $\operatorname{ri} C_1 \subseteq C_2 \subseteq \operatorname{cl} C_1$.
* Corollary 6.3.2, `Rockafellar.corollary_6_3_2`: every open set that meets $\operatorname{cl} C$
  meets $\operatorname{ri} C$.
* Corollary 6.3.3, `Rockafellar.corollary_6_3_3`: a convex subset of the relative boundary of a
  non-empty convex set $C$ has smaller dimension than $C$.
* Theorem 6.4, `Rockafellar.theorem_6_4`: the prolongation criterion for
  $z \in \operatorname{ri} C$.
* Corollary 6.4.1, `Rockafellar.corollary_6_4_1`: the prolongation criterion for
  $z \in \operatorname{int} C$.
* Theorem 6.5, `Rockafellar.theorem_6_5_cl`, `Rockafellar.theorem_6_5_ri`: the closure and, for
  finitely many sets, the relative interior of an intersection of convex sets whose relative
  interiors meet.
* Corollary 6.5.1, `Rockafellar.corollary_6_5_1_ri`, `Rockafellar.corollary_6_5_1_cl`: the same for
  the intersection with an affine set $M$ that meets $\operatorname{ri} C$.
* Corollary 6.5.2, `Rockafellar.corollary_6_5_2`: if $C_2 \subseteq \operatorname{cl} C_1$ is convex
  and not contained in the relative boundary of $C_1$, then $\operatorname{ri} C_2 \subseteq
  \operatorname{ri} C_1$.
* Theorem 6.6, `Rockafellar.theorem_6_6_ri`, `Rockafellar.theorem_6_6_cl`:
  $\operatorname{ri}(AC) = A(\operatorname{ri} C)$ and $\operatorname{cl}(AC) \supseteq
  A(\operatorname{cl} C)$ for a linear map $A$.
* Corollary 6.6.1, `Rockafellar.corollary_6_6_1`: $\operatorname{ri}(\lambda C) = \lambda
  \operatorname{ri} C$.
* Corollary 6.6.2, `Rockafellar.corollary_6_6_2_ri`, `Rockafellar.corollary_6_6_2_cl`:
  $\operatorname{ri}(C_1 + C_2) = \operatorname{ri} C_1 + \operatorname{ri} C_2$ and
  $\operatorname{cl}(C_1 + C_2) \supseteq \operatorname{cl} C_1 + \operatorname{cl} C_2$.
* Theorem 6.7, `Rockafellar.theorem_6_7_ri`, `Rockafellar.theorem_6_7_cl`: $\operatorname{ri}(A^{-1}
  C) = A^{-1}(\operatorname{ri} C)$ and $\operatorname{cl}(A^{-1} C) = A^{-1}(\operatorname{cl} C)$
  when $A^{-1}(\operatorname{ri} C) \neq \emptyset$.
* Theorem 6.8, `Rockafellar.theorem_6_8`: the relative interior of a convex set in
  $\mathbb{R}^{m+p}$, described through its slices.
* Corollary 6.8.1, `Rockafellar.corollary_6_8_1`: the relative interior of the cone over a non-empty
  convex set $C$ is $\{(\lambda, x) \mid \lambda > 0,\ x \in \lambda \operatorname{ri} C\}$.
* Theorem 6.9, `Rockafellar.theorem_6_9`: $\operatorname{ri} \operatorname{conv}(C_1 \cup C_2)$ is
  the union of the sets $\lambda_1 \operatorname{ri} C_1 + \lambda_2 \operatorname{ri} C_2$ with
  $\lambda_i > 0$ and $\lambda_1 + \lambda_2 = 1$.
* Unnumbered results at the end of the section, `Rockafellar.relint_prod`,
  `Rockafellar.closure_prod`, `Rockafellar.isRelativelyOpen_iInter`,
  `Rockafellar.isRelativelyOpen_add`, `Rockafellar.isCone_relint`, `Rockafellar.isCone_closure`:
  $\operatorname{ri}$ and $\operatorname{cl}$ of a direct sum, preservation of relative openness,
  and the relative interior and closure of a convex cone.

## Conventions

* The book's closure $\operatorname{cl} C$ is the Mathlib `closure C`.
* The book's interior $\operatorname{int} C$ is the Mathlib `interior C`.
* The book's relative interior $\operatorname{ri} C$ is the backbone notation `ri C`, which stands
  for the Mathlib `intrinsicInterior ℝ C`.
* As in Section 3, the space $\mathbb{R}^{m+p}$ is the product `Rn m × Rn p`. So Theorem 6.8 is
  stated on a product, and Corollary 6.8.1 on `ℝ × Rn n`.

## Implementation notes

* Theorem 6.2 is the point where the finite dimension of $\mathbb{R}^n$ is used, and the later
  sections depend on it. A non-empty convex set can have an empty relative interior in infinite
  dimensions; the positive cone of $\ell^2$ is the standard example. Sections 7, 9, 10, 11, 16, 18
  and 20 use Theorem 6.2 without saying so. Each statement here gets `FiniteDimensional ℝ (Rn n)`
  from the ambient instance, so no statement carries a hypothesis on the dimension, and the
  dependence is easy to miss.
* Theorems 6.6 and 6.7 are stated for a linear map. The book remarks that they also hold for affine
  transformations.

## Deviations from the source

* **Theorem 6.2**, affine hull and dimension of the closure (generalization). The book assumes that
  $C$ is convex. The statements hold for every set. See `Rockafellar.theorem_6_2_aff_cl` and
  `Rockafellar.theorem_6_2_dim_cl`.
* **Theorem 6.6**, closure (generalization). The book assumes that $C$ is convex. The inclusion
  $\operatorname{cl}(AC) \supseteq A(\operatorname{cl} C)$ holds for every set. See
  `Rockafellar.theorem_6_6_cl`.
* **Corollary 6.6.2**, closure (generalization). The book assumes that $C_1$ and $C_2$ are convex.
  The inclusion holds for all sets. See `Rockafellar.corollary_6_6_2_cl`.

## Not formalized

* **Theorem 6.9 for more than two sets.** The book states the theorem for $m$ sets
  $C_1, \dots, C_m$. Only the case $m = 2$ is formalized, as `Rockafellar.theorem_6_9`.
-/

namespace Rockafellar

open ConvexAnalysis
open scoped Pointwise

variable {n m p : ℕ} {C C₁ C₂ : Set (Rn n)}

/-! ### The relative interior, the relative boundary and relatively open sets -/

/-- **Section 6** of [rockafellar1970convex] (p. 44), definition of relative boundary. The
**relative boundary** of $C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ is the set $\operatorname{cl} C \setminus
\operatorname{ri} C$. -/
noncomputable def relbd (C : Set (Rn n)) : Set (Rn n) := closure C \ ri C

/-- The relative boundary of $C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ is the Mathlib `intrinsicFrontier ℝ C`. -/
theorem relbd_eq_intrinsicFrontier (C : Set (Rn n)) : relbd C = intrinsicFrontier ℝ C :=
  closure_sdiff_intrinsicInterior (𝕜 := ℝ) C

/-- A point $x$ is in the relative boundary of $C$ if and only if $x \in \operatorname{cl} C$ and
$x \notin \operatorname{ri} C$. -/
theorem mem_relbd_iff {x : Rn n} : x ∈ relbd C ↔ x ∈ closure C ∧ x ∉ ri C := Iff.rfl

/-- **Section 6** of [rockafellar1970convex] (p. 44), definition of relatively open set. A set
$C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ is **relatively open** if $\operatorname{ri} C = C$. -/
def IsRelativelyOpen (C : Set (Rn n)) : Prop := ri C = C

/-- A set $C$ is relatively open if and only if $\operatorname{ri} C = C$. This holds by definition.
-/
theorem isRelativelyOpen_iff : IsRelativelyOpen C ↔ ri C = C := Iff.rfl

/-- **Section 6** of [rockafellar1970convex] (p. 44). For every $C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$,
$\operatorname{ri} C \subseteq C \subseteq \operatorname{cl} C$. -/
theorem relint_subset_subset_closure (C : Set (Rn n)) : ri C ⊆ C ∧ C ⊆ closure C :=
  ⟨intrinsicInterior_subset, subset_closure⟩

/-- **Section 6** of [rockafellar1970convex] (p. 45). If $C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ has
$\operatorname{aff} C = \mathbb{R}^n$, then $\operatorname{ri} C = \operatorname{int} C$.

The book applies this to an $n$-dimensional convex set, for which
$\operatorname{aff} C = \mathbb{R}^n$. The statement needs no convexity. -/
theorem relint_eq_interior (h : affineSpan ℝ C = ⊤) : ri C = interior C :=
  intrinsicInterior_eq_interior h

/-- **Section 6** of [rockafellar1970convex] (p. 45). An affine set $M \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ is
relatively open. -/
theorem isRelativelyOpen_of_isAffineSet {M : Set (Rn n)} (h : IsAffineSet M) :
    IsRelativelyOpen M := by
  have key := AffineSubspace.intrinsicInterior_coe h.toAffineSubspace
  rw [h.coe_toAffineSubspace] at key
  exact key

/-- **Section 6** of [rockafellar1970convex] (p. 45). Every affine set $M \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ is
closed.

The book derives this from Corollary 1.4.1 and Theorem 1.3. Here it follows from the finite
dimension of $\mathbb{R}^n$. -/
theorem isClosed_of_isAffineSet {M : Set (Rn n)} (h : IsAffineSet M) : IsClosed M := by
  have key := h.toAffineSubspace.closed_of_finiteDimensional
  rwa [h.coe_toAffineSubspace] at key

/-- **Section 6** of [rockafellar1970convex] (p. 45). For every $C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$,
$\operatorname{cl} C \subseteq \operatorname{cl}(\operatorname{aff} C) = \operatorname{aff} C$.
So a line through two points of $\operatorname{cl} C$ lies in $\operatorname{aff} C$. -/
theorem closure_subset_affineSpan (C : Set (Rn n)) :
    closure C ⊆ (affineSpan ℝ C : Set (Rn n)) :=
  ConvexAnalysis.closure_subset_affineSpan C

/-! ### Theorem 6.1: the line segment principle -/

/-- **Theorem 6.1** of [rockafellar1970convex]. Let $C$ be a convex set in $\mathbb{R}^n$, let
$x \in \operatorname{ri} C$ and $y \in \operatorname{cl} C$. Then $(1 - \lambda) x + \lambda y$
belongs to $\operatorname{ri} C$ (and hence in particular to $C$) for $0 \le \lambda < 1$. -/
theorem theorem_6_1 (hC : Convex ℝ C) {x y : Rn n} (hx : x ∈ ri C) (hy : y ∈ closure C)
    {l : ℝ} (hl₀ : 0 ≤ l) (hl₁ : l < 1) : (1 - l) • x + l • y ∈ ri C :=
  Convex.segment_mem_relint hC hx hy hl₀ hl₁

/-! ### Theorem 6.2 -/

/-- **Theorem 6.2** of [rockafellar1970convex], convexity of the closure. If
$C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ is convex, then $\operatorname{cl} C$ is convex. -/
theorem theorem_6_2_convex_cl (hC : Convex ℝ C) : Convex ℝ (closure C) :=
  hC.closure

/-- **Theorem 6.2** of [rockafellar1970convex], convexity of the relative interior. If
$C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ is convex, then $\operatorname{ri} C$ is convex.

This follows from Theorem 6.1 with $y \in \operatorname{ri} C$. -/
theorem theorem_6_2_convex_ri (hC : Convex ℝ C) : Convex ℝ (ri C) :=
  Convex.relint hC

/-- **Theorem 6.2** of [rockafellar1970convex], affine hull of the closure. For every
$C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$, $\operatorname{aff}(\operatorname{cl} C) = \operatorname{aff} C$.

**Generalization.** The book assumes that $C$ is convex. This part holds for every set. -/
theorem theorem_6_2_aff_cl (C : Set (Rn n)) : affineSpan ℝ (closure C) = affineSpan ℝ C :=
  affineSpan_closure C

/-- **Theorem 6.2** of [rockafellar1970convex], affine hull of the relative interior. If
$C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ is convex, then $\operatorname{aff}(\operatorname{ri} C) =
\operatorname{aff} C$. -/
theorem theorem_6_2_aff_ri (hC : Convex ℝ C) : affineSpan ℝ (ri C) = affineSpan ℝ C :=
  Convex.affineSpan_relint hC

/-- **Theorem 6.2** of [rockafellar1970convex], dimension of the closure. For every
$C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$, $\dim(\operatorname{cl} C) = \dim C$.

**Generalization.** The book assumes that $C$ is convex. This part holds for every set.

The dimension is `Rockafellar.dim`. -/
theorem theorem_6_2_dim_cl (C : Set (Rn n)) : dim (closure C) = dim C := by
  rcases C.eq_empty_or_nonempty with rfl | hne
  · simp
  · rw [dim_of_nonempty (hne.mono subset_closure), dim_of_nonempty hne, ← direction_affineSpan,
      ← direction_affineSpan, affineSpan_closure]

/-- **Theorem 6.2** of [rockafellar1970convex], dimension of the relative interior. If
$C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ is convex, then $\dim(\operatorname{ri} C) = \dim C$.

The dimension is `Rockafellar.dim`. -/
theorem theorem_6_2_dim_ri (hC : Convex ℝ C) : dim (ri C) = dim C := by
  rcases C.eq_empty_or_nonempty with rfl | hne
  · simp
  · rw [dim_of_nonempty (Convex.relint_nonempty hC hne), dim_of_nonempty hne,
      ← direction_affineSpan, ← direction_affineSpan, Convex.affineSpan_relint hC]

/-- **Theorem 6.2** of [rockafellar1970convex], the parenthetical clause. If
$C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ is convex and $C \neq \emptyset$, then
$\operatorname{ri} C \neq \emptyset$.

This is the fact through which the results of Part II depend on the finite dimension of
$\mathbb{R}^n$. It is false in infinite dimensions, and no weaker hypothesis than finite
dimension suffices. -/
theorem theorem_6_2_nonempty (hC : Convex ℝ C) (hne : C.Nonempty) : (ri C).Nonempty :=
  Convex.relint_nonempty hC hne

/-! ### Theorem 6.3 and its corollaries -/

/-- **Theorem 6.3** of [rockafellar1970convex], closure of the relative interior. For any convex set
$C$ in $\mathbb{R}^n$, $\operatorname{cl}(\operatorname{ri} C) = \operatorname{cl} C$. -/
theorem theorem_6_3_cl_ri (hC : Convex ℝ C) : closure (ri C) = closure C :=
  Convex.closure_relint hC

/-- **Theorem 6.3** of [rockafellar1970convex], relative interior of the closure. For any convex set
$C$ in $\mathbb{R}^n$, $\operatorname{ri}(\operatorname{cl} C) = \operatorname{ri} C$. -/
theorem theorem_6_3_ri_cl (hC : Convex ℝ C) : ri (closure C) = ri C :=
  Convex.relint_closure hC

/-- **Section 6** of [rockafellar1970convex] (p. 46). If $C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ is convex, then
$\operatorname{ri}(\operatorname{ri} C) = \operatorname{ri} C$, that is, $\operatorname{ri} C$
is relatively open.

The book asserts this for arbitrary sets, before Theorem 6.3. The statement here is for convex
sets, and the backbone proves it through Theorem 6.3. -/
theorem isRelativelyOpen_relint (hC : Convex ℝ C) : IsRelativelyOpen (ri C) :=
  Convex.relint_relint hC

/-- **Corollary 6.3.1** of [rockafellar1970convex]. Let $C_1$ and $C_2$ be convex sets in
$\mathbb{R}^n$. Then $\operatorname{cl} C_1 = \operatorname{cl} C_2$ if and only if
$\operatorname{ri} C_1 = \operatorname{ri} C_2$. -/
theorem corollary_6_3_1 (h₁ : Convex ℝ C₁) (h₂ : Convex ℝ C₂) :
    closure C₁ = closure C₂ ↔ ri C₁ = ri C₂ :=
  Convex.closure_eq_iff_relint_eq h₁ h₂

/-- **Corollary 6.3.1** of [rockafellar1970convex], third condition. Let $C_1$ and $C_2$ be convex
sets in $\mathbb{R}^n$. Then $\operatorname{cl} C_1 = \operatorname{cl} C_2$ if and only if
$$\operatorname{ri} C_1 \subseteq C_2 \subseteq \operatorname{cl} C_1.$$
-/
theorem corollary_6_3_1_sandwich (h₁ : Convex ℝ C₁) (h₂ : Convex ℝ C₂) :
    closure C₁ = closure C₂ ↔ ri C₁ ⊆ C₂ ∧ C₂ ⊆ closure C₁ := by
  constructor
  · intro h
    refine ⟨?_, h ▸ subset_closure⟩
    rw [(Convex.closure_eq_iff_relint_eq h₁ h₂).1 h]
    exact intrinsicInterior_subset
  · rintro ⟨hsub, hsup⟩
    exact (Convex.closure_eq_of_relint_subset_of_subset_closure h₁ hsub hsup).symm

/-- **Corollary 6.3.2** of [rockafellar1970convex]. If $C$ is a convex set in $\mathbb{R}^n$, then
every open set which meets $\operatorname{cl} C$ also meets $\operatorname{ri} C$. -/
theorem corollary_6_3_2 (hC : Convex ℝ C) {U : Set (Rn n)} (hU : IsOpen U)
    (h : (U ∩ closure C).Nonempty) : (U ∩ ri C).Nonempty :=
  Convex.relint_inter_nonempty_of_isOpen hC hU h

/-- Let $C_1, C_2 \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ be convex, with $C_1$ non-empty,
$C_1 \subseteq \operatorname{cl} C_2$ and $\operatorname{aff} C_1 = \operatorname{aff} C_2$.
Then $C_1$ meets $\operatorname{ri} C_2$. This is the step that Corollary 6.3.3 needs.

A point of $\operatorname{ri} C_1$ has a ball of $\operatorname{aff} C_1 = \operatorname{aff}
C_2$ around it inside $C_1$, and $\operatorname{cl} C_2 = \operatorname{cl}(\operatorname{ri}
C_2)$ gives a point of $\operatorname{ri} C_2$ in that ball. -/
theorem inter_relint_nonempty_of_affineSpan_eq (h₁ : Convex ℝ C₁) (hne₁ : C₁.Nonempty)
    (h₂ : Convex ℝ C₂) (hsub : C₁ ⊆ closure C₂) (hspan : affineSpan ℝ C₁ = affineSpan ℝ C₂) :
    (C₁ ∩ ri C₂).Nonempty := by
  obtain ⟨z, hz⟩ := Convex.relint_nonempty h₁ hne₁
  have hzC₁ : z ∈ C₁ := intrinsicInterior_subset hz
  rw [mem_intrinsicInterior_iff] at hz
  obtain ⟨-, ε, hε, hball⟩ := hz
  have hzcl : z ∈ closure (ri C₂) := by
    rw [Convex.closure_relint h₂]
    exact hsub hzC₁
  obtain ⟨w, hw, hzw⟩ := Metric.mem_closure_iff.1 hzcl ε hε
  refine ⟨w, hball w ?_ (by rwa [dist_comm]), hw⟩
  rw [hspan]
  exact subset_affineSpan ℝ C₂ (intrinsicInterior_subset hw)

/-- **Corollary 6.3.3** of [rockafellar1970convex]. Let $C_2$ be a non-empty convex set in
$\mathbb{R}^n$, and let $C_1$ be a convex set contained in the relative boundary of $C_2$. Then
$\dim C_1 < \dim C_2$.

The dimension is `Rockafellar.dim`. The hypothesis $C_2 \neq \emptyset$ excludes the case
$C_1 = C_2 = \emptyset$, where both dimensions are $-1$. -/
theorem corollary_6_3_3 (h₁ : Convex ℝ C₁) (h₂ : Convex ℝ C₂) (hne₂ : C₂.Nonempty)
    (hsub : C₁ ⊆ relbd C₂) : dim C₁ < dim C₂ := by
  have hcl : C₁ ⊆ closure C₂ := fun x hx => (hsub hx).1
  have hdisj : ∀ x ∈ C₁, x ∉ ri C₂ := fun x hx => (hsub hx).2
  rcases C₁.eq_empty_or_nonempty with rfl | hne₁
  · rw [dim_empty, dim_of_nonempty hne₂]
    omega
  have hle : vectorSpan ℝ C₁ ≤ vectorSpan ℝ C₂ := by
    have h := vectorSpan_mono ℝ hcl
    rwa [← direction_affineSpan, ← direction_affineSpan, affineSpan_closure,
      direction_affineSpan, direction_affineSpan] at h
  rw [dim_of_nonempty hne₁, dim_of_nonempty hne₂, Nat.cast_lt]
  refine lt_of_le_of_ne (Submodule.finrank_mono hle) fun heq => ?_
  have hdir : vectorSpan ℝ C₁ = vectorSpan ℝ C₂ := Submodule.eq_of_le_of_finrank_eq hle heq
  have hspan : affineSpan ℝ C₁ = affineSpan ℝ C₂ := by
    refine AffineSubspace.ext_of_direction_eq ?_ ?_
    · rw [direction_affineSpan, direction_affineSpan, hdir]
    · obtain ⟨x, hx⟩ := hne₁
      exact ⟨x, subset_affineSpan ℝ C₁ hx,
        ConvexAnalysis.closure_subset_affineSpan C₂ (hcl hx)⟩
  obtain ⟨x, hx₁, hx₂⟩ := inter_relint_nonempty_of_affineSpan_eq h₁ hne₁ h₂ hcl hspan
  exact hdisj x hx₁ hx₂

/-! ### Theorem 6.4: the prolongation criterion -/

/-- **Theorem 6.4** of [rockafellar1970convex]. Let $C$ be a non-empty convex set in $\mathbb{R}^n$.
Then $z \in \operatorname{ri} C$ if and only if, for every $x \in C$, there exists a $\mu > 1$
such that $(1 - \mu) x + \mu z$ belongs to $C$. -/
theorem theorem_6_4 (hC : Convex ℝ C) (hne : C.Nonempty) {z : Rn n} :
    z ∈ ri C ↔ ∀ x ∈ C, ∃ μ > (1 : ℝ), (1 - μ) • x + μ • z ∈ C :=
  Convex.mem_relint_iff_prolong hC hne

/-- **Corollary 6.4.1** of [rockafellar1970convex]. Let $C$ be a convex set in $\mathbb{R}^n$. Then
$z \in \operatorname{int} C$ if and only if, for every $y \in \mathbb{R}^n$, there exists some
$\varepsilon > 0$ such that $z + \varepsilon y \in C$. -/
theorem corollary_6_4_1 (hC : Convex ℝ C) {z : Rn n} :
    z ∈ interior C ↔ ∀ y : Rn n, ∃ ε : ℝ, 0 < ε ∧ z + ε • y ∈ C :=
  Convex.mem_interior_iff_absorbs hC

/-! ### Theorem 6.5: intersections -/

/-- **Theorem 6.5** of [rockafellar1970convex], the closure formula. Let $C_i$ be a convex set in
$\mathbb{R}^n$ for $i \in I$, where $I$ is an arbitrary index set. Suppose that the sets
$\operatorname{ri} C_i$ have at least one point in common. Then
$$\operatorname{cl} \bigcap_{i \in I} C_i = \bigcap_{i \in I} \operatorname{cl} C_i.$$
-/
theorem theorem_6_5_cl {ι : Type*} {C : ι → Set (Rn n)} (hC : ∀ i, Convex ℝ (C i))
    (h : (⋂ i, ri (C i)).Nonempty) : closure (⋂ i, C i) = ⋂ i, closure (C i) :=
  Convex.closure_iInter hC h

/-- **Theorem 6.5** of [rockafellar1970convex], the relative interior formula. Let $C_i$ be a convex
set in $\mathbb{R}^n$ for $i \in I$, where $I$ is finite. Suppose that the sets
$\operatorname{ri} C_i$ have at least one point in common. Then
$$\operatorname{ri} \bigcap_{i \in I} C_i = \bigcap_{i \in I} \operatorname{ri} C_i.$$

The finiteness of $I$ cannot be dropped. The book gives the counterexample of the intervals
$[0, 1 + \alpha]$ for $\alpha > 0$. -/
theorem theorem_6_5_ri {ι : Type*} [Finite ι] {C : ι → Set (Rn n)} (hC : ∀ i, Convex ℝ (C i))
    (h : (⋂ i, ri (C i)).Nonempty) : ri (⋂ i, C i) = ⋂ i, ri (C i) :=
  Convex.relint_iInter hC h

/-- **Corollary 6.5.1** of [rockafellar1970convex], the relative interior. Let $C$ be a convex set
in $\mathbb{R}^n$, and let $M$ be an affine set (such as a line or a hyperplane) which contains
a point of $\operatorname{ri} C$. Then $\operatorname{ri}(M \cap C) = M \cap \operatorname{ri}
C$. -/
theorem corollary_6_5_1_ri (hC : Convex ℝ C) {M : Set (Rn n)} (hM : IsAffineSet M)
    (h : (M ∩ ri C).Nonempty) : ri (M ∩ C) = M ∩ ri C := by
  have key := Convex.relint_inter_affine (M := hM.toAffineSubspace) hC
    (by rwa [hM.coe_toAffineSubspace])
  rwa [hM.coe_toAffineSubspace] at key

/-- **Corollary 6.5.1** of [rockafellar1970convex], the closure. Let $C$ be a convex set in
$\mathbb{R}^n$, and let $M$ be an affine set which contains a point of $\operatorname{ri} C$.
Then $\operatorname{cl}(M \cap C) = M \cap \operatorname{cl} C$. -/
theorem corollary_6_5_1_cl (hC : Convex ℝ C) {M : Set (Rn n)} (hM : IsAffineSet M)
    (h : (M ∩ ri C).Nonempty) : closure (M ∩ C) = M ∩ closure C := by
  have key := Convex.closure_inter_affine (M := hM.toAffineSubspace) hC
    (by rwa [hM.coe_toAffineSubspace])
  rwa [hM.coe_toAffineSubspace] at key

/-- **Corollary 6.5.2** of [rockafellar1970convex]. Let $C_1$ be a convex set in $\mathbb{R}^n$. Let
$C_2$ be a convex set contained in $\operatorname{cl} C_1$ but not entirely contained in the
relative boundary of $C_1$. Then $\operatorname{ri} C_2 \subseteq \operatorname{ri} C_1$. -/
theorem corollary_6_5_2 (h₁ : Convex ℝ C₁) (h₂ : Convex ℝ C₂) (hsub : C₂ ⊆ closure C₁)
    (hnb : ¬ C₂ ⊆ relbd C₁) : ri C₂ ⊆ ri C₁ := by
  refine Convex.relint_subset_relint_of_subset_closure h₁ h₂ hsub ?_
  by_contra hem
  refine hnb fun x hx => ⟨hsub hx, fun hx₁ => hem ⟨x, hx, hx₁⟩⟩

/-! ### Theorem 6.6: images under a linear transformation -/

/-- **Theorem 6.6** of [rockafellar1970convex], the relative interior. Let $C$ be a convex set in
$\mathbb{R}^n$, and let $A$ be a linear transformation from $\mathbb{R}^n$ to $\mathbb{R}^m$.
Then $\operatorname{ri}(AC) = A(\operatorname{ri} C)$. -/
theorem theorem_6_6_ri (hC : Convex ℝ C) (A : Rn n →ₗ[ℝ] Rn m) : ri (A '' C) = A '' ri C :=
  Convex.relint_image hC A

/-- **Theorem 6.6** of [rockafellar1970convex], the closure. Let $C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$, and let
$A$ be a linear transformation from $\mathbb{R}^n$ to $\mathbb{R}^m$. Then
$\operatorname{cl}(AC) \supseteq A(\operatorname{cl} C)$.

**Generalization.** The book assumes that $C$ is convex. The inclusion follows from the
continuity of $A$ and holds for every set. -/
theorem theorem_6_6_cl (C : Set (Rn n)) (A : Rn n →ₗ[ℝ] Rn m) :
    A '' closure C ⊆ closure (A '' C) :=
  image_closure_subset C A

/-- **Corollary 6.6.1** of [rockafellar1970convex]. For any convex set $C$ in $\mathbb{R}^n$ and any
real number $\lambda$, $\operatorname{ri}(\lambda C) = \lambda \operatorname{ri} C$. -/
theorem corollary_6_6_1 (hC : Convex ℝ C) (l : ℝ) : ri (l • C) = l • ri C :=
  Convex.relint_smul hC l

/-- **Section 6** of [rockafellar1970convex] (p. 49). For any sets $C_1 \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ and
$C_2 \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m$, the relative interior of the direct sum is the direct sum of the
relative interiors:
$$\operatorname{ri}(C_1 \oplus C_2) = \operatorname{ri} C_1 \oplus \operatorname{ri} C_2.$$

The book calls this formula elementary. The direct sum is the product `D₁ ×ˢ D₂`, and the
statement needs no convexity. -/
theorem relint_prod (D₁ : Set (Rn n)) (D₂ : Set (Rn m)) :
    ri (D₁ ×ˢ D₂) = ri D₁ ×ˢ ri D₂ :=
  intrinsicInterior_prod_eq D₁ D₂

/-- **Section 6** of [rockafellar1970convex] (p. 49). For any sets $C_1 \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ and
$C_2 \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m$,
$$\operatorname{cl}(C_1 \oplus C_2) = \operatorname{cl} C_1 \oplus \operatorname{cl} C_2.$$
-/
theorem closure_prod (D₁ : Set (Rn n)) (D₂ : Set (Rn m)) :
    closure (D₁ ×ˢ D₂) = closure D₁ ×ˢ closure D₂ :=
  closure_prod_eq

/-- **Corollary 6.6.2** of [rockafellar1970convex], the relative interior. For any convex sets $C_1$
and $C_2$ in $\mathbb{R}^n$,
$$\operatorname{ri}(C_1 + C_2) = \operatorname{ri} C_1 + \operatorname{ri} C_2.$$
-/
theorem corollary_6_6_2_ri (h₁ : Convex ℝ C₁) (h₂ : Convex ℝ C₂) :
    ri (C₁ + C₂) = ri C₁ + ri C₂ :=
  Convex.relint_add h₁ h₂

/-- **Corollary 6.6.2** of [rockafellar1970convex], the closure. For any sets $C_1$ and $C_2$ in
$\mathbb{R}^n$,
$$\operatorname{cl}(C_1 + C_2) \supseteq \operatorname{cl} C_1 + \operatorname{cl} C_2.$$

**Generalization.** The book assumes that $C_1$ and $C_2$ are convex. The inclusion holds for
all sets.

Corollary 9.1.1 sharpens this inclusion to an equality. -/
theorem corollary_6_6_2_cl (D₁ D₂ : Set (Rn n)) :
    closure D₁ + closure D₂ ⊆ closure (D₁ + D₂) :=
  closure_add_subset D₁ D₂

/-! ### Theorem 6.7: inverse images under a linear transformation -/

/-- **Theorem 6.7** of [rockafellar1970convex], the relative interior. Let $A$ be a linear
transformation from $\mathbb{R}^n$ to $\mathbb{R}^m$. Let $C$ be a convex set in $\mathbb{R}^m$
such that $A^{-1}(\operatorname{ri} C) \neq \emptyset$. Then $\operatorname{ri}(A^{-1} C) =
A^{-1}(\operatorname{ri} C)$. -/
theorem theorem_6_7_ri {D : Set (Rn m)} (hD : Convex ℝ D) (A : Rn n →ₗ[ℝ] Rn m)
    (h : (A ⁻¹' ri D).Nonempty) : ri (A ⁻¹' D) = A ⁻¹' ri D :=
  Convex.relint_preimage hD A h

/-- **Theorem 6.7** of [rockafellar1970convex], the closure. Let $A$ be a linear transformation from
$\mathbb{R}^n$ to $\mathbb{R}^m$. Let $C$ be a convex set in $\mathbb{R}^m$ such that
$A^{-1}(\operatorname{ri} C) \neq \emptyset$. Then $\operatorname{cl}(A^{-1} C) =
A^{-1}(\operatorname{cl} C)$. -/
theorem theorem_6_7_cl {D : Set (Rn m)} (hD : Convex ℝ D) (A : Rn n →ₗ[ℝ] Rn m)
    (h : (A ⁻¹' ri D).Nonempty) : closure (A ⁻¹' D) = A ⁻¹' closure D :=
  Convex.closure_preimage hD A h

/-! ### Theorem 6.8: slices of a convex set in a product -/

/-- **Theorem 6.8** of [rockafellar1970convex]. Let $C$ be a convex set in $\mathbb{R}^{m+p}$. For
each $y \in \mathbb{R}^m$, let $C_y = \{z \in \mathbb{R}^p \mid (y, z) \in C\}$, and let
$D = \{y \mid C_y \neq \emptyset\}$. Then $(y, z) \in \operatorname{ri} C$ if and only if
$y \in \operatorname{ri} D$ and $z \in \operatorname{ri} C_y$.

The space $\mathbb{R}^{m+p}$ is the product `Rn m × Rn p`. -/
theorem theorem_6_8 {C : Set (Rn m × Rn p)} (hC : Convex ℝ C) (y : Rn m) (z : Rn p) :
    (y, z) ∈ ri C ↔
      y ∈ ri {u : Rn m | {w : Rn p | (u, w) ∈ C}.Nonempty} ∧ z ∈ ri {w : Rn p | (y, w) ∈ C} := by
  have hD : {u : Rn m | {w : Rn p | (u, w) ∈ C}.Nonempty} = Prod.fst '' C := by
    ext u
    constructor
    · rintro ⟨w, hw⟩
      exact ⟨(u, w), hw, rfl⟩
    · rintro ⟨q, hq, rfl⟩
      exact ⟨q.2, hq⟩
  rw [hD]
  exact Convex.mem_relint_prod_iff hC

/-- **Corollary 6.8.1** of [rockafellar1970convex], the cone over a convex set. For
$C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$, this is the set
$$K = \{(\lambda, x) \mid \lambda > 0,\ x \in \lambda C\}$$
of the positive multiples of $\{(1, x) \mid x \in C\}$. For convex $C$ it is the convex cone in
$\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$ generated by $\{(1, x) \mid x \in C\}$ (`Rockafellar.mem_coneOver_iff`,
`Rockafellar.convex_coneOver`).

A cone in the sense of [rockafellar1970convex] need not contain the origin, so $K$ consists of
the positive multiples of $\{(1, x) \mid x \in C\}$ and nothing more. The height $\lambda$ is
the first component, in `ℝ × Rn n`. -/
def coneOver (C : Set (Rn n)) : Set (ℝ × Rn n) := {q | 0 < q.1 ∧ q.2 ∈ q.1 • C}

/-- A pair $q$ belongs to the cone `Rockafellar.coneOver C` if and only if $q = \lambda (1, x)$ for
some $\lambda > 0$ and $x \in C$. By Corollary 2.6.3 this is the smallest convex cone that
contains $\{(1, x) \mid x \in C\}$, when $C$ is convex. -/
theorem mem_coneOver_iff {q : ℝ × Rn n} :
    q ∈ coneOver C ↔ ∃ l : ℝ, 0 < l ∧ ∃ x ∈ C, l • ((1 : ℝ), x) = q := by
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨hq, x, hx, hxq⟩
    exact ⟨q.1, hq, x, hx, Prod.ext (by simp) (by simpa using hxq)⟩
  · rintro ⟨l, hl, x, hx, rfl⟩
    exact ⟨by simpa using hl, x, hx, by simp⟩

/-- If $C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ is convex, then the cone `Rockafellar.coneOver C` is convex. It is
the intersection of the cone of the backbone, which contains the origin, with the open
half-space $\{(\lambda, x) \mid \lambda > 0\}$. -/
theorem convex_coneOver (hC : Convex ℝ C) : Convex ℝ (coneOver C) := by
  have hhalf : Convex ℝ {q : ℝ × Rn n | 0 < q.1} :=
    convex_halfSpace_gt (LinearMap.fst ℝ ℝ (Rn n)).isLinear 0
  have hset : coneOver C = insert 0 (coneOver C) ∩ {q : ℝ × Rn n | 0 < q.1} := by
    ext q
    constructor
    · exact fun hq => ⟨Set.mem_insert_of_mem _ hq, hq.1⟩
    · rintro ⟨hq | hq, hq'⟩
      · rw [hq] at hq'
        simp at hq'
      · exact hq
  rw [hset]
  exact _root_.Convex.inter (convex_cone_prodMk_one hC) hhalf

/-- **Corollary 6.8.1** of [rockafellar1970convex]. Let $C$ be a non-empty convex set in
$\mathbb{R}^n$, and let $K$ be the convex cone in $\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$ generated by
$\{(1, x) \mid x \in C\}$. Then $\operatorname{ri} K$ consists of the pairs $(\lambda, x)$ such
that $\lambda > 0$ and $x \in \lambda \operatorname{ri} C$.

The cone $K$ is `Rockafellar.coneOver C`. -/
theorem corollary_6_8_1 (hC : Convex ℝ C) (hne : C.Nonempty) :
    ri (coneOver C) = {q : ℝ × Rn n | 0 < q.1 ∧ q.2 ∈ q.1 • ri C} := by
  have hconv0 : Convex ℝ (insert 0 (coneOver C)) := convex_cone_prodMk_one hC
  have hri : ri (insert 0 (coneOver C)) = {q : ℝ × Rn n | 0 < q.1 ∧ q.2 ∈ q.1 • ri C} :=
    Convex.relint_cone_prodMk_one hC hne
  have hsub : ri (insert 0 (coneOver C)) ⊆ coneOver C := by
    rw [hri]
    rintro q ⟨h1, h2⟩
    exact ⟨h1, Set.smul_set_mono intrinsicInterior_subset h2⟩
  have hsup : coneOver C ⊆ closure (insert 0 (coneOver C)) :=
    (Set.subset_insert _ _).trans subset_closure
  have hcl := Convex.closure_eq_of_relint_subset_of_subset_closure hconv0 hsub hsup
  rw [← hri]
  exact (Convex.closure_eq_iff_relint_eq (convex_coneOver hC) hconv0).1 hcl

/-! ### Theorem 6.9 -/

/-- **Theorem 6.9** of [rockafellar1970convex], the case of two sets. Let $C_1$ and $C_2$ be
non-empty convex sets in $\mathbb{R}^n$, and let $C_0 = \operatorname{conv}(C_1 \cup C_2)$. Then
$$\operatorname{ri} C_0 = \bigcup \{\lambda_1 \operatorname{ri} C_1 + \lambda_2
  \operatorname{ri} C_2 \mid \lambda_1 > 0,\ \lambda_2 > 0,\ \lambda_1 + \lambda_2 = 1\}.$$

The book states the theorem for $m$ sets $C_1, \dots, C_m$. Only the case $m = 2$ is formalized. -/
theorem theorem_6_9 (h₁ : Convex ℝ C₁) (hne₁ : C₁.Nonempty) (h₂ : Convex ℝ C₂)
    (hne₂ : C₂.Nonempty) :
    ri (convexHull ℝ (C₁ ∪ C₂)) =
      ⋃ (l₁ : ℝ) (l₂ : ℝ) (_ : 0 < l₁) (_ : 0 < l₂) (_ : l₁ + l₂ = 1),
        (l₁ • ri C₁ + l₂ • ri C₂) := by
  rw [Convex.relint_convexHull_union h₁ hne₁ h₂ hne₂]
  ext x
  simp only [Set.mem_iUnion, Set.mem_Ioo, exists_prop]
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨a, ⟨ha₀, ha₁⟩, hx⟩
    exact ⟨1 - a, a, by linarith, ha₀, by ring, hx⟩
  · rintro ⟨l₁, l₂, hl₁, hl₂, hl, hx⟩
    refine ⟨l₂, ⟨hl₂, by linarith⟩, ?_⟩
    rw [show (1 : ℝ) - l₂ = l₁ by linarith]
    exact hx

/-!
### The relatively open sets of Section 6

The book closes the section (p. 49) with the remarks that the operations above preserve relative
openness, and that the relative interior and the closure of a convex cone are again convex cones.
-/

/-- **Section 6** of [rockafellar1970convex] (p. 49). The class of relatively open convex sets is
preserved under finite intersections, provided that the intersection is non-empty: if
$C_i \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ is a relatively open convex set for each $i$ in a finite index set
$I$ and $\bigcap_{i \in I} C_i \neq \emptyset$, then $\bigcap_{i \in I} C_i$ is relatively open.

For relatively open sets, the condition of Theorem 6.5 that the relative interiors meet is the
non-emptiness of the intersection. -/
theorem isRelativelyOpen_iInter {ι : Type*} [Finite ι] {C : ι → Set (Rn n)}
    (hC : ∀ i, Convex ℝ (C i)) (hopen : ∀ i, IsRelativelyOpen (C i))
    (h : (⋂ i, C i).Nonempty) : IsRelativelyOpen (⋂ i, C i) := by
  have hopen' : ∀ i, ri (C i) = C i := fun i => (isRelativelyOpen_iff).1 (hopen i)
  have heq : (⋂ i, ri (C i)) = ⋂ i, C i := Set.iInter_congr hopen'
  refine isRelativelyOpen_iff.2 ?_
  rw [Convex.relint_iInter hC (heq ▸ h), heq]

/-- **Section 6** of [rockafellar1970convex] (p. 49). The class of relatively open convex sets is
preserved under addition: if $C_1, C_2 \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ are relatively open and convex,
then $C_1 + C_2$ is relatively open. -/
theorem isRelativelyOpen_add (h₁ : Convex ℝ C₁) (h₂ : Convex ℝ C₂)
    (ho₁ : IsRelativelyOpen C₁) (ho₂ : IsRelativelyOpen C₂) : IsRelativelyOpen (C₁ + C₂) := by
  refine isRelativelyOpen_iff.2 ?_
  rw [Convex.relint_add h₁ h₂, isRelativelyOpen_iff.1 ho₁, isRelativelyOpen_iff.1 ho₂]

/-- **Section 6** of [rockafellar1970convex] (p. 50). The relative interior of a convex cone is a
convex cone; this statement gives the cone property.

It follows from Corollary 6.6.1, because a convex set $C$ is a cone exactly when $\lambda C = C$
for every $\lambda > 0$ (`Rockafellar.isCone_iff_smul_set_eq`). -/
theorem isCone_relint (hK : IsCone C) (hC : Convex ℝ C) : IsCone (ri C) := by
  rw [isCone_iff_smul_set_eq] at hK ⊢
  intro a ha
  rw [← Convex.relint_smul hC a, hK a ha]

/-- **Section 6** of [rockafellar1970convex] (p. 50). The closure of a convex cone is a convex cone;
this statement gives the cone property: if $C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ is a cone, then
$\operatorname{cl} C$ is a cone.

The statement needs no convexity. -/
theorem isCone_closure (hK : IsCone C) : IsCone (closure C) := by
  rw [isCone_iff_smul_set_eq]
  exact smul_closure_eq_of_isCone hK

end Rockafellar
